## 4. Classes

### `FlagSpec`

`@dataclass`, no base classes. *Lines 220–229.* A simple threshold-comparison flag: it fires when `flagDf[col] < thresholds[thresh_key]` (direction `"below"`) or `> ` (direction `"above"`).

| Attribute | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `name` | `str` | Boolean column written to `flagDf`, e.g. `"flag_low_es"` |
| `col` | `str` | Column in `flagDf` to compare, e.g. `"avg_es"` |
| `thresh_key` | `str` | Key into the `thresholds` dict, e.g. `"es"` |
| `direction` | `str` | `"below"` or `"above"` |
| `label` | `str` | Short header used in Excel/PDF flag columns |
| `description` | `str` | Longer text shown on the Legend sheet |

No methods. Evaluated in `getFlagDf` at lines 2305–2310.

### `CustomFlagSpec`

`@dataclass`, no base classes. *Lines 231–237.* A flag computed by an arbitrary function rather than a threshold comparison.

| Attribute | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `name` | `str` | Boolean column written to `flagDf` |
| `fn` | `Callable` | `fn(row: pd.Series, thresholds: dict) -> bool`, applied row-wise |
| `label` | `str` | Short header |
| `description` | `str` (default `""`) | Legend text |

Three flags use this: `no_improvement_es`, `no_improvement_gr`, `clinical_incident`.

### `FlaggingConfig`

*Lines 1649–1909.* No base classes. Full specification for one flagging run: which flags, which sheets, which thresholds, which tier classification. The docstring gives a quickstart (use a preset, `.copy(...)` to override one field, build from scratch, `.describe()` to inspect).

