## 7. Gotchas and known issues

**Fixed 2026-08-18 — two silent-wrong-answer bugs (kept here as a record)**

- **Role and patient detail were matched by LABEL only.** `role_counts.get("Operator")` and
  `pd_counts.get("I saw a patient")` never matched the v3 CODE spelling (`O`, `ISP`), so on a
  code-shaped pull `operator_count`, `pct_operator` and `patient_seen` came out **zero** and
  `low_pt_seen` fired on the wrong students. `patient_fta` was accidentally safe because it matched
  on the substring `"FTA"`, present in both spellings. Fixed by `normalizeRole` /
  `normalizePatientDetail`. **Re-running an old workbook will now produce different Operator /
  % Oper / Pt Seen numbers — that is the fix, not a regression.**
- **`_build_clinical_incidents_sheet` selected `assessor_name`, `role` and `clinic` unguarded.**
  One missing column raised `KeyError`, `saveFlagDfToExcel2` caught and printed it, and the
  workbook arrived with **no incident sheet at all**. Context columns are now optional. (The reason
  the sheet was *empty* even when it built is a separate bug in the v3 loader — see
  `boh2_dds2_dds3_utils.clinicalIncidentSqlExpr`.)

**Two "score" numbers that legitimately disagree (2026-08-18)**

- Summary's `Score` is **form-weighted** (each form's item mean, then averaged across forms — a
  one-item form counts as much as a twelve-item one). The Section Score Pivot's section cells and
  `Overall (per item)` are **item-weighted**. On the DDS2 FHY 2026 run they differed by a mean of
  0.021, a max of 0.064, and moved one student **43 rank places**; at the 0.63 cutoff `low_score`
  went from 8 flagged to 6. `Overall (per item)` is the section means weighted by their own item
  counts — **not** the plain average of the section cells, which is off by up to 0.107.
- `FlaggingConfig.scoreWeighting` switches which one `avg_score` (and so `low_score` and the score
  threshold) uses. **`score_slope` / `first_third_*` / `last_third_*` deliberately do NOT follow** —
  a form is the natural point on a time series — so under `per_item` the Scale Trends headers
  `Score Avg (per item)` and `Score Slope (per form)` intentionally disagree.
- Which column Summary is using is rendered into the pivot's banner and footnote **at write time**
  from the config. Don't bake it into a header — it goes stale the moment the switch is flipped.

**Filters and dates (2026-08-18)**

- `filterCohortDfByPeriod` and `dropIgnoredDates` compare the **Melbourne-local** date, never the
  raw UTC one. DASH stores UTC, so a late-afternoon Melbourne session sits on the previous UTC day
  and a naive `.dt.date` puts it in the wrong semester at the 15 June boundary.
- `parseIgnoreDates` assumes **day-first** for ambiguous slash dates: `03/02/2026` is 3 February.
- An unparseable ignore date **raises**; one that matches no forms prints a **WARNING**. Both are
  deliberate — a silently-ineffective exclusion is worse than none, because the workbook looks like
  it was applied.
- `ignoreDates` removes a **whole day**, AM and PM. `Config.xlsx`'s "15/07/2026 AM technical
  problems" note therefore still can't be honoured.
- The role/patient code maps are **duplicated** in `boh2_dds2_dds3_utils` (`ROLE_LABELS_FALLBACK`,
  `PATIENT_DETAIL_LABELS_FALLBACK`). A new DASH code must be added to both.

**Duplicate / dead definitions**

- **`_itemCodeCounts` is defined twice** — line 57 and line 2385. The later definition wins for every caller, so the line-57 version (which returns a *typed* empty DataFrame and does not filter `"scale*"` codes) is dead code that reads as if it were live. Anyone editing the version at line 57 will see no effect.
- **`_levelDistribution` (lines 72–77) is never called.** `getFlagDf` uses its own nested `_lvl_dist` (line 2181), which differs in behaviour: it returns `0.0` for empty input where `_levelDistribution` returns `np.nan`.
- `has_ci` (line 2205) is computed and never used. `pct_patient_seen` (line 2202) is stored on `flagDf` but appears on no sheet.
- Unused imports: `os` (line 6), `re` (line 7), `variableUtils` (line 11), `List`/`Optional` (line 13), `GradientFill` (line 15), `FORMAT_NUMBER_00` (line 16), and 8 of the 10 names imported from `Utils` on line 14 (`createTable`, `addPlotImage`, `getBannerDrawer`, `getmodeArgs`, `readDf`, `runDdl`, `toInt`, `autoFitColumns`). Only `_loadSectionMapping` and `_mergeSection` are used. The module-level `matplotlib.pyplot` import (line 10) is also redundant — `_render_donut` re-imports it locally.

**Truncated function**

- **`_build_operator_by_clinic_sheet` stops mid-statement at line 1423.** The colour loop at lines 1415–1423 iterates `[("Operator", …), ("FTA", …)]` but only has a body for `FTA` (`val > avg × 1.5` → red); the `Operator` branch does nothing, there is no amber/green case, the `clinic_avg` "Cohort Avg" rows computed at lines 1373–1377 are never written to the sheet, and no column widths or `freeze_panes` are set. The two blank lines before the next section (1424–1426) suggest an edit was lost.

**Threshold formulas that don't match their documentation**

- `thresholds["forms"]` (line 2280) and `thresholds["min_pt_seen"]` (line 2287) **ignore `config.sdMultiplier`** — they are always `mean − 1·SD`, while `es/gr/ts/cs/ps/score` use `mean − sdMultiplier·SD`. Presets that set `sdMultiplier = 1.5` (`sim_standard`, `boh2_sim`, `boh2_clinic`, `dds2_clinic`, `dds3_clinic`) therefore mix two different auto rules in one run.
- `thresholds["forms"]` uses `int(...)` rather than `round(...)`, truncating toward zero, and can go negative for a cohort with a large SD (a negative minimum means `low_form_count` can never fire).
- `FlaggingConfig.describe()` (lines 1880–1892) prints "auto = cohort mean - 1 SD" for every auto threshold, which is wrong whenever `sdMultiplier != 1.0`, and does not mention the borderline method for ES/TS/CS/PS/Score at all in that table (it is described separately at lines 1868–1871).
- `thresholds["sd_multiplier"] = config.sdMultiplier or 1.0` — a deliberate `sdMultiplier = 0` silently becomes `1.0` (same trick inside `_auto`, line 2248).

**Flag semantics that surprise people**

- **NaN never fires a flag.** Students with missing scale data or a single form (so `NaN` slopes) escape `low_*` and `declining_*` entirely, and will look "clean" on the Summary sheet.
- **`high_es_lvl2` cannot fire at its default threshold** of `maxEsLvl2Pct = 100.0` — no percentage exceeds 100. Every preset that lists the flag (including `dds3_clinic`, which also puts it in `lowTierFlags`) leaves it at 100.0, so the `ES L2%` column is permanently green.
- **`no_improvement_*` uses `<=` with `improvementMargin = 0.0`**, so a flat trajectory counts as "no improvement"; and because `third = max(1, n // 3)` (line 2175), a student with 1–2 forms has identical first/last thirds and is guaranteed to fire it.
- `resolve_tiers()` silently reclassifies a key present in **both** tier lists as HIGH (with a printed warning only), and ignores unknown keys.
- A flag in `config.flags` but in neither tier list still fires and still shows in Excel — coloured **red** by `_build_summary_sheet` (the "unclassified defaults to severe" branch at lines 528–531) — while counting toward neither risk column. The only signal is a printed warning at line 2328.

**Hard-coded values that are not configurable**

- Level-distribution colour cut-offs (20 % / 10 % bad, 50 % good) — lines 662–670.
- GR pivot colour cut-offs (2.5 / 3.0 / 4.0) — lines 786–791.
- Comparison-sheet `pct_of_cohort` cut-offs (0.5 / 0.9) — lines 720–723.
- Reportable-item cut-offs (50 % / 80 % of cohort average) — lines 1237–1242 and 2634–2637.
- `_colorPivotLowCells` bottom-quartile default 0.25, and its `threshold > 0` guard which silently disables colouring for sparse columns.
- `DDS3_REPORTABLE_ITEMS` is dated *2026-07* and sourced from *DDS3 Reportable Item numbers.xlsx*; it is baked into the module. `_build_item_scale_donut_sheet` (line 1472) and both PDF sections (lines 2562, 2594) use it unconditionally for **any** cohort. The other two DDS3 builders read `getattr(config, "reportableItems", None)`, but `FlaggingConfig` never defines that attribute, so the getattr is always `None` — the hook does not work.
- The notebook's output filename hard-codes the year: `f"{cohort} {formType} … Flagging (2026).xlsx"`.

**Error handling that hides problems**

- `saveFlagDfToExcel2` wraps every sheet builder in `except Exception` and only prints (line 2670). A cohortDf missing, say, `clinical_incident` produces a workbook that is silently short a sheet. If *every* sheet fails, the `ExcelWriter` context exits with no worksheets, which openpyxl will reject at save time.
- `saveFlagDfToExcel2` iterates `config.sheets` directly instead of `config.resolve_sheets()`, so the `KeyError`-with-help-text path in `resolve_sheets` never runs from the main entry point — unknown sheet keys are just printed and skipped.
- `saveFlagDfToPdf`'s two DDS3 sections each swallow all exceptions (lines 2589, 2650).
- `_buildItemCountPivot` (line 1039) and `_itemCodeCounts` (line 2395) swallow JSON parse errors with bare `except Exception`, silently dropping the row's item codes.

**Inconsistent contracts**

- `mappingFile` means two different things. The sheet builders and `_buildCountPivot`/`_buildGrPivot` pass it to `_loadSectionMapping`, so it must be a **path**. `getFlagDf`'s Clinic branch does `mappingFile = mappingFile or _loadSectionMapping(None)` and then passes it as the `mappingDf` argument of `_mergeSection` (line 2368), so it must be a **DataFrame**. Passing a path to `getFlagDf` will break the section merge.
- Three different "which rows count as Operator" rules coexist: `_buildItemCountPivot` and `_buildItemScaleDist` treat a **blank** role as Operator (`not in ("", "Operator")` → skip, lines 1034 and 1099); `_build_item_scale_donut_sheet` requires `role.strip().lower() == "operator"` (line 1485); `getFlagDf` counts `role == "Operator"` exactly (line 2195).
- Two different name-resolution rules coexist: `getFlagDf`'s per-student aggregation takes `grp["student_name"].iloc[0]` (line 2161) and `_buildCountPivot` uses `drop_duplicates()`, while `_canonicalNameMap` (most-frequent spelling) is used only for `compDf` and `_buildItemCountPivot`. A student with inconsistent name spellings can therefore show one name on the Summary sheet and another on the Comparison / Reportable Items sheets.
- `_buildItemCountPivot` sorts item columns with `key=lambda x: int(x)` (line 1075) — a non-numeric reportable item code would raise `ValueError`.

**Performance / output size**

- `_build_item_scale_donut_sheet` renders one matplotlib figure per non-empty (student × item) cell and embeds it as a PNG. For a full DDS3 cohort × 51 item codes this is potentially thousands of images — slow to build and a very large `.xlsx`. `_render_donut` also calls `matplotlib.use("Agg")` on every call, changing the backend for the whole process.
- `getFlagDf`, `_buildGrPivot`, `_buildItemCountPivot` and `_buildItemScaleDist` all use row-by-row `iterrows()` over the form-level frame.

**Cosmetic**

- The Legend sheet sets column D to width **0** (line 980–981), hiding it.
- `_build_legend_sheet` and `_build_item_scale_donut_sheet` create their sheets with `writer.book.create_sheet`, so they are appended at the end of the workbook regardless of their position in `config.sheets`.
- `_hdr_style` inside `saveFlagDfToPdf` takes `n_cols` and `col_widths` and uses neither.
- `_build_scale_trends_sheet` and `_build_level_distribution_sheet` write every value twice — once via `to_excel`, once in the styling loop.
- Indentation of the `high_es_lvl2` entry in `FLAG_REGISTRY` (lines 317–320) does not match the surrounding entries.
